const title = "How Long Do Dryers Last? — Same Day";
const description = "How long a dryer lasts: about 13 years on average. What shortens it (the vent is the big one), gas vs electric, the fire-safety angle, and the honest repair-vs-replace math from a repair tech.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How long does a dryer last on average?",
    a: "About 13 years is the commonly referenced average, from the NAHB home-component life-expectancy data. It's an older reference (originally 2007), so treat it as a general benchmark rather than a precise figure. Dryers are mechanically simpler than washers — a motor, a heat source, a belt, rollers, and a thermostat — so a well-kept one often reaches or passes that mark. The single biggest thing that shortens a dryer's life isn't the machine at all: it's a neglected exhaust vent."
  },
  {
    q: "Do gas and electric dryers last about the same?",
    a: "Roughly, yes — both land near that 13-year average, and the mechanical parts (motor, drum, rollers, belt) are similar. The difference is the heat source: an electric dryer uses a heating element, while a gas dryer uses a burner, igniter, and gas valve. Gas dryers have a couple more parts that can fail, but they're not inherently shorter-lived. What matters far more than gas versus electric is whether the exhaust vent is kept clear, because a restricted vent makes either type run hot, work harder, and wear out faster."
  },
  {
    q: "Why does the vent matter so much for how long a dryer lasts?",
    a: "A clogged vent is a dryer's worst enemy on two fronts. First, longevity: when lint blocks the exhaust, heat can't escape, so the dryer runs hotter and longer every load, which cooks the thermostats, heating element, and motor and shortens the machine's life. Second, safety: trapped lint and heat are a leading cause of household dryer fires. Cleaning the lint filter every load and the full exhaust vent once or twice a year is the single highest-value thing you can do for both the dryer's lifespan and your home's safety."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing an old dryer?",
    a: "Usually yes, more so than with most appliances, because dryers are simple and the common repairs are affordable — a typical dryer repair runs roughly $100 to $300 depending on the part. A thermal fuse, heating element, igniter, belt, or set of rollers is a clear repair even on an older machine. The math only tilts toward replacement when an older dryer needs a major part like the motor or drum bearing and the repair approaches the cost of a new unit. Our techs give you the real number and an honest read for $89, waived with the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "How can I make my dryer last longer?",
    a: "Keep the airflow clear and the load sensible. Clean the lint filter before every load — a filmy filter (from fabric softener) chokes airflow even when it looks clean, so wash it occasionally. Have the full exhaust vent cleaned once or twice a year, more if it's a long run. Don't overload the drum, which strains the motor and belt. And if the dryer takes more than one cycle to dry a normal load, treat that as an early warning that the vent is restricted — fixing it then protects the machine before the heat damages a part."
  }
];

