<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">About 13 years — and a clear vent is what gets you there.</h2>

    <p><strong>A clothes dryer lasts about 13 years on average, per the NAHB home-component life-expectancy data — a useful benchmark, though an older one (originally 2007), so treat it as general rather than exact. Dryers are mechanically simple, so a well-maintained one often reaches or passes that mark. The single biggest factor isn't the machine, the brand, or gas-versus-electric — it's the exhaust vent. A clogged vent makes any dryer run hot and work harder every load, which cooks the parts and cuts years off its life (and is a leading cause of dryer fires). Clean the filter every load and the vent once or twice a year, and you've done the most important thing. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>Because dryers are simple and the common repairs are cheap, they're one of the more repair-worthy appliances in the house — a broken dryer is usually worth fixing well into its second decade. The exception is a major part late in life, which is where an honest repair-or-replace read matters.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHAT AFFECTS IT -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · What decides where yours lands</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The vent first, then usage.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>The exhaust vent (the big one)</h3>
      <p>This is the factor that moves the number most. When lint blocks the exhaust, heat can't escape, so the dryer runs hotter and longer every cycle — and that heat is exactly what kills thermostats, heating elements, and motors early. A long or crushed vent run makes it worse. Keeping the vent clear is the highest-value thing you can do for a dryer's lifespan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>How hard it's worked</h3>
      <p>A dryer running several loads a day ages faster than one doing a few loads a week, and overloading strains the motor and belt. Sensible loads and not cramming the drum add years.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas vs electric</h3>
      <p>Both land near the same average. Electric dryers heat with an element; gas dryers use a burner, igniter, and valve — a couple more parts that can fail, but not a shorter life. The vent matters far more than the fuel type.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Maintenance</h3>
      <p>Cleaning the lint filter every load (and washing off softener film occasionally), clearing the vent yearly, and keeping the machine level all add life. Neglect on any of these gives it back.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Note on brand: unlike some appliances, there isn't reliable public per-brand dryer failure data to rank longevity, so we won't claim one badge lasts longer than another. Maintenance and the vent decide it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 REPAIR VS REPLACE -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Repair or replace?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Dryers are usually worth fixing.</h2>

    <p>A typical dryer repair runs roughly <strong>$100 to $300</strong> depending on the part, and most dryer faults are the cheap, common ones — a thermal fuse, heating element, igniter, thermostat, belt, or rollers. Against a machine you're keeping, those are an easy repair even past 10 years.</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Repair (almost always)</h3>
      <p>Thermal fuse, heating element or igniter, thermostat, belt, rollers, door switch, or a control glitch. These are affordable and quick, and they don't signal the machine is done.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Weigh it carefully</h3>
      <p>An older dryer (past ~12 years) facing a failed drive motor or a worn drum bearing, where the repair climbs toward the price of a new unit. That's the one case replacement often wins — and we'll tell you straight.</p>
    </div>

    <p>One thing our techs always check first on a "not heating" or "takes forever" call: the vent. A blocked vent mimics a failed part and, if it caused an overheat, may be why a fuse or thermostat blew in the first place — replacing the part without clearing the vent just means it fails again. See typical part pricing on <a href="/price-list/dryer-repair-cost/">dryer repair costs</a>. <strong>$89 diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 EXTEND LIFE -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Get more years out of it</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Airflow, airflow, airflow.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter every load.</strong> Wash it occasionally to remove fabric-softener film, which chokes airflow invisibly.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the full exhaust vent once or twice a year.</strong> More often for long runs — this protects both the machine and against fire.</li>
      <li><strong>Don't overload the drum.</strong> Cramming it strains the motor and belt every cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat "more than one cycle to dry" as a warning.</strong> It usually means a restricted vent — fix it before the heat damages a part.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep it level.</strong> A rocking dryer wears the drum rollers and bearing faster.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 WHEN TO CALL + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Not heating, taking forever, or noisy? Get it looked at.</h2>

    <p>A dryer that won't heat, takes multiple cycles, or grinds and squeals is telling you a part has gone or the vent is choked — and either way, catching it early keeps a cheap repair cheap. Our techs verify the vent, test the heating and safety components, and give you a straight repair-or-replace read based on the machine's age.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/dryer-repair/">dryer repair</a> hub, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a>, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/takes-too-long/">dryer takes too long</a>, and <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a>. LG owners with a d80/d90/d95 airflow warning: see the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-d80-error/">LG d80/d90/d95</a> guide. A restricted vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/lg-dryer-repair/">LG</a>, <a href="/brands/samsung-dryer-repair/">Samsung</a>, and <a href="/brands/whirlpool-dryer-repair/">Whirlpool</a> dryer repair. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
